---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-food-meal-plan`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D Meal Plan Ideas',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D Meal Plan', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="High-Vitamin D Meal Plan Ideas: Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner That Hit the RDA"
  description="Sample days of eating that reach the 600–800 IU RDA from food alone — breakfast, lunch, dinner, and snack ideas built around salmon, fortified dairy, mushrooms, and eggs."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-food-meal-plan", label: "Meal Plan Ideas" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">High-Vitamin D Meal Plan Ideas</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Hitting the vitamin D RDA (600–800 IU) from food alone is possible but requires
      deliberate choices — vitamin D content of most everyday foods is small. Here are
      sample days that get there without supplementation. These are food ideas, not
      prescriptions; supplementation is still often the more reliable strategy.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Day 1 — the salmon dinner day (~940 IU)</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> 2 large eggs (80 IU) + 1 cup vitamin D-fortified milk (120 IU) + toast = 200 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> Salad with 3 oz canned tuna (150 IU), 1 oz vitamin D-fortified cheese, vinaigrette = 200 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> 4 oz baked wild salmon (600 IU), roasted vegetables, quinoa = 600 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Snack:</strong> Yoghurt cup (~40 IU if fortified) = 40 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Total: ~1,040 IU</strong></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Day 2 — the omelette-and-mushroom day (~610 IU)</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> 3-egg omelette (120 IU) with 1 cup UV-exposed mushrooms (200 IU), whole-grain toast = 320 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> Sardine salad — 3 oz canned sardines (170 IU) on greens with olive oil = 170 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> Roast chicken with vitamin D-fortified milk-based mashed potatoes (~100 IU) = 100 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Snack:</strong> Fortified orange juice (100 IU) = 100 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Total: ~690 IU</strong></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Day 3 — the trout dinner (~810 IU)</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> Bowl of vitamin D-fortified cereal (100 IU) with milk (120 IU) = 220 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> 2 hard-boiled eggs (80 IU) + fortified cottage cheese (~60 IU) + fruit = 140 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> 4 oz rainbow trout (500 IU), rice, greens = 500 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Snack:</strong> Almonds and dried fruit = 0 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Total: ~860 IU</strong></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vegan variant (harder — approximately 400 IU max from food)</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> UV-exposed mushrooms omelette-substitute + fortified plant milk (120 IU) = 320 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> Salad with fortified nutritional yeast + fortified plant milk = 50 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> Vegan bowl with UV-exposed mushrooms and fortified plant milk = 200 IU.</li>
      <li><strong>Total: ~570 IU</strong> — often under the RDA. Supplementation with lichen D3 is standard for vegans in northern climates.</li>
      <li>See our <a href="/vitamin-d-for-vegans" class="text-amber-700 underline">vegan page</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Top vitamin D foods to work into meals</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Wild salmon, 4 oz cooked: 600–1,000 IU</li>
      <li>Farmed salmon, 4 oz: 300–500 IU</li>
      <li>Rainbow trout, 4 oz: 500–700 IU</li>
      <li>Canned sardines, 3 oz: 170 IU</li>
      <li>Canned tuna, 3 oz: 130 IU</li>
      <li>Egg, 1 large: 40 IU</li>
      <li>UV-exposed maitake mushrooms, 1 cup: 786 IU</li>
      <li>Standard fortified milk, 1 cup: 100–120 IU</li>
      <li>Fortified plant milk, 1 cup: 100–120 IU</li>
      <li>Fortified breakfast cereal, 1 serving: 40–100 IU</li>
      <li>Fortified orange juice, 1 cup: 100 IU</li>
      <li>Beef liver, 3 oz: 42 IU</li>
      <li>Cod liver oil, 1 tsp: 450 IU</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The realistic take</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Most adults do not eat oily fish daily, mushrooms are only vitamin D-rich if
      UV-exposed (rare in supermarkets), and fortified products provide modest
      amounts. Food-based vitamin D adequacy is possible with deliberate planning
      but not automatic. Supplementation remains the more reliable strategy,
      especially in winter or if you don't eat fish. See the full
      <a href="/vitamin-d-foods" class="text-amber-700 underline">foods list</a>.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> These are food ideas, not prescriptions;
      vitamin D content varies by species, farming practices, cooking method, and
      product formulation.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-food-meal-plan" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
